          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP319-Laurier Heights

&lt;strong&gt;GPSr reads N 53° 31.155 W 113° 33.934&lt;/strong&gt;

Cap in passable condition. Most numbers readable.

Email with info sent.

GeoGuider and I were making the most of the Canada Day long weekend.  We picked up several BCPs, including this one. TFTC OFTH.&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="614189333">
          <groundspeak:date>2016-07-02T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="1095439">GeoGuider</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP 319 Laurier Heights

Posted:  N53° 31.157     W113° 33.932

My GPSr:  N53° 31.155     W113° 33.934&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;j0ckser and I were off doing errands today in a part of the city that we usually don’t travel.  Is there a better excuse to find a brasscap?  Being a holiday weekend, the traffic was light and we were able to dash over to the traffic circle without trouble.  Once there, we wandered this way and that way until we found the prize.  This one is painted white and is nestled under the SE corner of a spruce tree marked with more flagging tape than you can shake a stick at… but no, our eyes were on the ground as we wandered around looking for possible “spots” so it took a while.  One of these days, I’m going to start looking for the obvious first.  DUH!  Thanks for the brass cap OTFH.&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
